---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";

const title = "Pikos vs Obsidian";
const description = "Obsidian is the better tool for linking and building a knowledge base. Pikos does notes, tasks and a calendar as one thing. An honest comparison, including what Pikos doesn't do.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-8 py-20">

    <a href="/compare" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-neutral-400 transition-colors hover:text-primary-500 dark:text-neutral-500 dark:hover:text-primary-400">
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m15 18-6-6 6-6"/></svg>
      Compare
    </a>

    <article>
      <h1 class="mt-8 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs Obsidian</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400">
        Both keep your notes on your own machine as files you can read without the app. They part company on what a note is for.
      </p>

      <Prose class="mt-10">

        <h2>The short version</h2>

        <p>
          Obsidian is built for connecting ideas. Links between notes, a graph of how they relate, and a plugin for anything it doesn't do out of the box. It's the best tool there is for building a body of knowledge you'll come back to for years.
        </p>

        <p>
          Pikos is built for the week you're in. A page is a note, a task and a calendar entry at the same time. You write it, tick it off, and drop it on Thursday, and it stays one page rather than becoming three things in three apps.
        </p>

        <h2>Stay with Obsidian if</h2>

        <p>
          You link notes heavily and use the graph. Pikos has search but no backlinks and no graph view, and that's a deliberate gap rather than a to-do.
        </p>

        <p>
          You rely on plugins. Obsidian's ecosystem is enormous and Pikos has none. There's no plugin API and won't be one until the data model stops moving.
        </p>

        <p>
          You need your notes on a phone today. Obsidian has mobile apps and a sync service now. The Pikos iPhone app is being built and isn't out.
        </p>

        <p>
          You write in Markdown and want the file on disk to be the source of truth. Obsidian's files are plain Markdown in a folder. Pikos keeps a SQLite file and exports Markdown, which is a different promise: you can always get the files out, but the folder isn't what the app reads.
        </p>

        <h2>Try Pikos if</h2>

        <p>
          Tasks in Obsidian have never quite worked for you. Checkboxes in Markdown plus a plugin to collect them is a workaround, and it shows when you want a due date, a priority and a sort order.
        </p>

        <p>
          You keep a calendar in a second app and copy things between them. Pikos puts the week beside the editor, and you can drag a page onto a day.
        </p>

        <p>
          You'd like it all in one window. Obsidian plus a couple of plugins gets close to what Pikos does, and if you've already built that setup and you like it, there's no reason to move. Pikos is for when you'd rather not maintain the setup at all.
        </p>

        <h2>What Pikos doesn't do</h2>

        <p>
          No backlinks, no graph, no plugins, no phone app yet, no sync yet, and a much smaller community to ask when something breaks. Obsidian is nine years old and Pikos is not.
        </p>

        <h2>Moving a vault across</h2>

        <p>
          Pikos reads an Obsidian vault directly from disk, with no export step on the Obsidian side. Frontmatter, wikilinks, callouts and image attachments all come through. Point it at the folder you'd open in Obsidian and it walks the whole thing, skipping <code>.obsidian</code> and any other hidden directory.
        </p>

        <p>
          The full walkthrough is on the <a href="/import/obsidian" class="font-medium text-primary-500 hover:underline dark:text-primary-400">Obsidian import page</a>. Your vault stays where it is; the import copies rather than moves, so you can run both for a while and decide.
        </p>

      </Prose>

      <div class="mt-16 rounded-lg border border-neutral-200 bg-neutral-50 px-6 py-6 dark:border-neutral-800 dark:bg-neutral-900">
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-300">
          The feature-by-feature table, and comparisons with Notion, NotePlan, TickTick and Things, are on the <a href="/compare" class="font-medium text-primary-500 hover:underline dark:text-primary-400">comparison page</a>. Pikos is a <a href="/download" class="font-medium text-primary-500 hover:underline dark:text-primary-400">free download</a> with no account, so you can run both and keep the one that fits.
        </p>
      </div>

    </article>

  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>
